The old man sat by the window, his eyes fixed on the sky. A fluffy cloud drifted lazily, and he smiled, thinking of his wife. "I love you," he said softly, his voice gentle.

His wife, a kind woman, smiled back. "I love you too," she replied, her eyes crinkling.

But the old man was not happy. He had been a man who often exaggerated his feelings. He would tell stories about his wife's happiness, but he never shared his own. He would pretend to be happy, even when he was not.

One day, the old man noticed that his wife was sad. She looked at him, her eyes filled with tears. "I'm so sorry," she said.

The old man felt a pang of sadness. He thought of his wife and his own feelings. He realized that love is not about being happy, but about being present. It is about showing up, even when it is hard.

He decided to change his behavior. He would not exaggerate his feelings. He would not pretend to be happy. He would be honest, even when it was difficult.

